claimed: deploy-secret-schemes -- stage 4 (specs/deployment.md +
specs/conf.md): the four resolvers okay-conf has been promising.

`Tools.forScheme` already maps sops:/aws-sm:/gcp-sm:/azure-kv: and the
doctor already tells an operator to install those binaries -- while no
resolver implements any of them. The repository promises something it
does not deliver, and that is the reason this goes before another
render target.

Each shells out to the VENDOR CLI rather than an SDK: no new
dependency, the operator's own profile / SSO / login already works,
and `doctor` already checks for exactly those binaries.

The decision I expect to matter, and it INVERTS okay-deploy's rule: a
failed resolver must NOT carry the command's output. Shell.failure
includes the last lines because those are renderers; a secret
resolver's stdout may BE the secret, so a failure names the command
and the exit code and stops.

Gate: sops end to end for real -- a container with sops and age, a
generated key, an encrypted file, decrypted THROUGH the resolver via a
shim at the same absolute path inside and out. The three cloud
managers have no account here, so what is tested is the command each
builds and the named refusal when the binary is absent -- said in the
spec rather than implied.

Files: okay-conf scala-jvm-native + scala-js Platform/Schemes, tests,
specs/conf.md + specs/deployment.md, boards.
